WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.464
OT security architecture makes connectivity intentional.

00:00:03.464 --> 00:00:10.623
Enterprise IT and control networks should be separated where their functions and risk differ.

00:00:10.623 --> 00:00:14.548
Separation does not mean eliminating every connection.

00:00:14.548 --> 00:00:20.322
It means permitting required communication through controlled, documented, and monitored pathways.

00:00:20.322 --> 00:00:25.171
An industrial DMZ can buffer enterprise and site operations.

00:00:25.171 --> 00:00:35.101
Services such as jump hosts, remote-access brokers, update repositories, logging gateways, and data replication can terminate or mediate cross-boundary activity.

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:42.260
Enterprise users should not reach controllers directly simply because a route is technically possible.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.262
Conduits should use least-privilege connectivity.

00:00:45.262 --> 00:00:51.497
Define approved sources, destinations, protocols, direction, business or operational purpose, and owner.

00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:58.656
Review administrative traffic, authentication, backup, updates, and vendor support as carefully as production data.

00:00:58.656 --> 00:01:02.120
Temporary paths need expiration and removal.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:07.431
Monitoring placement should align with trust boundaries and operational value.

00:01:07.431 --> 00:01:11.819
Visibility at enterprise-to-OT boundaries can reveal attempted movement.

00:01:11.819 --> 00:01:19.439
Monitoring within site and control areas can identify unexpected devices, engineering actions, and unusual communications.

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.827
Passive techniques may be preferable around sensitive devices.

00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:26.829
Architecture should also preserve resilience.

00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:34.911
Understand which functions continue if an enterprise service, DMZ, network link, or site server is lost.

00:01:34.911 --> 00:01:43.918
Design local control, redundancy, and recovery so a cyber containment decision does not create an avoidable safety problem.

00:01:43.918 --> 00:01:55.233
Modern OT may include cloud analytics, wireless systems, industrial Internet of Things devices, and vendor-managed services that do not follow a simple hierarchy.

00:01:55.233 --> 00:02:02.392
Extend the architecture with data-flow diagrams, identity paths, external dependencies, and current asset records.

00:02:02.392 --> 00:02:10.936
The objective is not a perfect diagram; it is controlled communication supported by evidence and operational understanding.
